-- The two joins of a quantified obligation, on the BPMN exporter.
--
-- `the tenancy` is the BARRIER: every tenant signs, and when the last of them
-- has, one delivery follows. `receipts` is the FORK: each tenant who pays gets
-- a receipt of their own. They are the reference page's own pair
-- (doc/reference/regulative/every-run-example.l4) with the traces left out.
--
-- Until 2026-09-15 these two lowered to byte-identical BPMN and a
-- byte-identical fidelity report: L4.StateGraph.extractDeonton never read
-- Deonton.join, so the exporter could not tell them apart and the report that
-- exists to list the losses did not list this one. The barrier is now a
-- multi-instance TASK, whose own completion is the barrier; the fork is a
-- multi-instance SUB-PROCESS enclosing the member's whole continuation, which
-- is what makes once-per-member drawable. The goldens are the witness that the
-- pair stays distinguishable.
--
-- `receipts and delivery` is the third one, and it exists because of a defect
-- it would have caught. The fork's breach end must NOT be an error end — an
-- error end ends every active thread in the process, so one tenant's breach
-- would cancel the tenants who had not breached. The first fix demoted the
-- SHARED breach terminal in place, guarded by "only where the fork is its sole
-- feeder", which is true of every other fixture here and false as soon as
-- anything else can breach. This rule is that case: a fork beside a PARTY
-- obligation, both LEST BREACH, so the landlord's breach keeps the shared
-- error end (under RAND one operand's breach is the whole contract's) while
-- the fork gets its own plain one.

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Landlord HAS name IS A STRING
    Tenant   HAS name IS A STRING

DECLARE Action IS ONE OF
    Sign    HAS signer IS AN Actor
    Deliver HAS who    IS AN Actor
    Pay     HAS payer  IS AN Actor, payee IS AN Actor, amount IS A NUMBER
    Receipt HAS issuer IS AN Actor, to    IS AN Actor, amount IS A NUMBER

theLandlord MEANS Landlord OF "Ms Ng"
alice       MEANS Tenant OF "Alice"
bob         MEANS Tenant OF "Bob"
carol       MEANS Tenant OF "Carol"
tenants     MEANS LIST alice, bob, carol

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`the tenancy` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN tenants
        MUST   Sign t
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  (PARTY theLandlord MUST Deliver theLandlord WITHIN 5)
        LEST   BREACH

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`receipts` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN tenants
        MUST   Pay t theLandlord amount
        WITHIN 7
        UPON   EACH
        HENCE  (PARTY theLandlord
                    MUST   Receipt theLandlord t amount
                    WITHIN 5)
        LEST   BREACH BY t

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`receipts and delivery` MEANS
    (EVERY Tenant t IN tenants
        MUST   Pay t theLandlord amount
        WITHIN 7
        UPON   EACH
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH BY t)
    RAND
    (PARTY theLandlord
        MUST   Deliver theLandlord
        WITHIN 14
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH)
